---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image-to-200kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={200} inputLabel="Drop the images that need to come in under 200 KB" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>200 KB is the web-performance sweet spot</h2>
    <p>
      Most page-speed guidance agrees: keep each image under about 200 KB and the whole page under one
      or two megabytes. At 200 KB a full-width hero photo still looks very good, and on a mid-range
      mobile connection it loads in less than a second. Above that, you pay for detail nobody looks at.
      Largest Contentful Paint, the Core Web Vital that fails most often, is usually this image.
    </p>

    <h2>Resize before you compress</h2>
    <p>
      This gives the largest gain, and people often skip it. A 4000 px photo shown in an 800 px column
      has twenty-five times more pixels than the layout can show. Limit the width first with the field
      in the options. The quality search then has much less work, and the same 200 KB gives a much
      sharper image. As a starting point:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Placement</th><th>Sensible max width</th><th>Typical size at quality 80</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Full-width hero</td><td>1920 px</td><td>180–300 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Content column image</td><td>1200 px</td><td>90–160 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Card or listing thumbnail</td><td>600 px</td><td>25–50 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Avatar</td><td>200 px</td><td>6–12 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Email newsletter image</td><td>1200 px</td><td>100–180 KB</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Metadata is a bigger share than you think</h2>
    <p>
      A camera JPEG has an EXIF block, often an embedded colour profile, and a full JPEG thumbnail of
      itself. Together these often add 20–80 KB, up to 40% of a 200 KB budget, for data that no browser
      shows. This tool re-encodes from raw pixels, so it drops all of it. To see the metadata first, use
      the <a href="/image-metadata-viewer">metadata viewer</a>. To remove only the metadata and keep the
      pixels, use <a href="/remove-exif-data">Remove EXIF Data</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>JPEG, WebP or AVIF for the web?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>WebP: supported by all current browsers, usually 25–35% smaller than JPEG at the same quality. The default choice for a modern site.</li>
      <li>JPEG: universal, including email clients and older CMS previews. Use it when you do not control where the image is shown.</li>
      <li>PNG: only for screenshots, logos, charts and flat colour. A photo saved as PNG is often ten times larger than necessary.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Batch a whole gallery</h2>
    <p>
      Drop in the whole folder. Each image gets its own search to the 200 KB target, with its own
      progress row, and “Download all as .zip” puts the results in one file. There is no file cap and
      no daily quota, because the encoding runs on your CPU. For a large gallery, also set the width
      limit: each attempt then encodes fewer pixels, and the run is several times faster.
    </p>

    <h2>Checking your work</h2>
    <p>
      The result card shows the byte count, the dimensions and the quality the search chose. If the
      quality is 90 or above, your target is generous and you can go lower. If it is under 55, the image
      is too large for the budget: crop or resize it, and do not accept the artefacts. Local processing
      gives you this feedback without another upload.
    </p>

    <h2>Other targets</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb">100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-500kb">500 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb">1 MB</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
